In a discussion, James outlined findings on direct climate impacts for the U.S. National Climate Assessment categorized into economic, agricultural, health, environmental, and socioeconomic sectors. Key points include rising economic losses from extreme weather events (10.1073/pnas.2017524118), declines in agricultural crop yields due to heat stress (10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.122.060746), and increased drought risks affecting water management (10.1029/2024EF004577). Health impacts encompass worsening air quality leading to respiratory issues (10.1016/j.envres.2020.109206). Coastal regions are threatened by sea-level rise (10.1016/j.ijdrr.2023.104020), while biodiversity suffers from habitat changes (10.1073/pnas.2201345119). The synthesis emphasizes that comprehensive mitigation and adaptation strategies are crucial to address these interconnected challenges effectively.
